From: danlip@proton.cyc.com (Dan Lipofsky)
Subject: bug in read command in sh under OSF1 V3.0
Date: 1997/03/21
Message-ID: <5gumt5$3qm@news3.texas.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 227344941
Organization: Cycorp, Inc. (Austin, TX)
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.unix.shell,comp.unix.admin


I have discovered a bug (I dont think anyone would call it a feature)
in the functioning of the "read" command in sh under OSF1 V3.0

I have compared it to Linux 2.0, SunOS (4.1.3, 4.1.4, and 5.5),
AIX 4.1, and HP-UX A.09.01.  This only occurs under OSF1 V3.0.

If you read in a line which contains '\\t' (or similar things)
it reads in as '\t', except under OSF1, where it reads in as '\\t'.

If you read in a line which contains '\t' [the preceding character isnt a \]
it reads in as 't' under all systems.

This basically means that there is no way to read in a line so
the result will contain '\t', since every attempt will yield
either `\\t` or `t`.

Does anyone know or a fix or a work-around?
Does anyone know where this bug should be reported?

Here is a script which demonstrates the problem:
$ cat /home/danlip/tmp/foo
\tfoobar\n
$ cat /home/danlip/tmp/bar
\\tfoobar\\n
$ cat ./test.sh 
#! /bin/sh

test1='\\tfoobar\\n'
test2='\tfoobar\n'
test3='tfoobarn'

read foo < /home/danlip/tmp/foo

if [ $foo = $test1 ]; then
    echo "read matches test1"
elif [ $foo = $test2 ]; then
    echo "read matches test2"
elif [ $foo = $test3 ]; then
    echo "read matches test3"
else
    echo "read - NO MATCH"
fi

read bar < /home/danlip/tmp/bar

if [ $bar = $test1 ]; then
    echo "read matches test1"
elif [ $bar = $test2 ]; then
    echo "read matches test2"
elif [ $bar = $test3 ]; then
    echo "read matches test3"
else
    echo "read - NO MATCH"
fi
$ ./test.sh 
read matches test3
read matches test1

The results above are from OSF1.
On all other operating systems I get:
$ ./test.sh
read matches test3
read matches test2

Thanks for any help,
Dan



