Here is the latest DIGITAL Q&A document on Java.  
------------------------------------------------

Q: What is Digital's position on Java?

A: Digital believes Java is an important technology on the Internet, and is 
   committed to providing Java support on its  Alpha and VAX 
   platforms.  We believe that Alpha's superior performance will make it an 
   excellent platform for developing and executing Java applications.  

Q: What is the status of Java at Digital?

A: Digital executed a license in September 1996 with Sun Microsystems, Inc. for 
   Java technology and rights to produce a high-performance implementation 
   of the Java virtual machine, including a Just-in-Time (JIT) compiler.  
   Java is a technology which is well-suited for development of 
   Internet applications, and will play an important role in Digital's 
   strategy to bring enterprise strength Internet solutions to its customers.

   Digital currently provides the Java Development Kit V1.0.2 on Digital 
   UNIX, which includes the interpreter-based Java virtual machine and the 
   javac compiler.  The virtual machine (VM) is the runtime environment for 
   Java applets and applications and includes Class Libraries and Native 
   Methods.  

   Digital plans to provide the JDK V1.1, along with a Java Just-in-Time (JIT) 
   compiler.  A JIT converts Java byte code to native high performance Alpha 
   machine code at run-time, resulting in significantly greater performance 
   than interpreter-based technology.

   Java applets can execute today on Digital's Alpha and VAX platforms 
   via the Netscape Navigator web browser products for Digital UNIX and 
   OpenVMS.  In addition, Microsoft's Internet Explorer Java-enabled web 
   browser is supported on Windows NT Alpha.

Q: What is the status of Java on Digital UNIX?

A: Digital released the Java Development Kit V1.0.2 for Digital UNIX V4.0a 
   on October 31, 1996.  Digital provides the base development 
   tools, and we plan to partner with leading software tools providers for a 
   full-functioned Java development environment.

   The JDK 1.0.2 is available via no-charge download from the following web
   site:  http://www.unix.digital.com/products/internet/java.html.

   We plan to release a beta version of the JDK V1.1 in early April 1997.
   A JIT compiler is being planned for this beta release.

Q: Will Digital provide Java on Windows NT Alpha?

A: Digital and Microsoft are working closely to deliver Java support for
   Windows NT Alpha. Final shipping details for specific components are not
   available at this time.

   Java applets can run on Windows NT Alpha inside the Netscape Navigator and 
   Microsoft Internet Explorer browser products.

Q: Will Digital provide Java on OpenVMS?

A: We will provide a JDK 1.1 for OpenVMS in the Summer 1997 timeframe.  Our 
   current plans are to provide a JDK on both Alpha and VAX, along with a JIT 
   compiler for Alpha.

Q: What is the status of StrongARM with Java?

A: The StrongARM SA-110 microprocessor currently runs JavaOS and has the
   highest published scores for the CaffeineMark 2.01 interpreted Java
   applet performance benchmark.  StrongARM scores are higher than those of 
   a Pentium Pro and Sparc Ultra at a fraction of the cost.




