From the man page:

Substitutes the string repl in place of the first instance
of the extended regular expression ERE in string in and return the
number of substitutions.

That is, the second argument of 'sub' and 'gsub' is a *string*, not a
regular expression. 
So your gsub line should read:
	gsub(/,/,"",$2)
The '1' appears to be the result of a match of the second regular
expression - in the case of // this always matches. But putting /_/ (for
example) in as the second parameter puts a '0' in the spaces, and
putting /a/ in puts a '0' in the first example line, and a 1 in the
third line.
Hmmm...

